skill: SQL Basics

@ Queries and Filtering | B
Which statement retrieves all columns from a table named employees?
= SELECT * FROM employees;
- GET ALL FROM employees;
- SELECT employees;
- FETCH * employees;
? SELECT * FROM table_name returns every column of every row.

@ Queries and Filtering | B
Which clause sorts the rows in a result set?
= ORDER BY
- SORT BY
- GROUP BY
- ARRANGE BY
? ORDER BY sorts ascending by default. Add DESC for descending order.

@ Queries and Filtering | B
Which operator tests whether a value lies within an inclusive range?
= BETWEEN
- RANGE
- WITHIN
- LIKE
? x BETWEEN 10 AND 20 is true when x is greater than or equal to 10 and less than or equal to 20.

@ Queries and Filtering | I
Which condition correctly finds rows where the email column has no value?
= WHERE email IS NULL
- WHERE email = NULL
- WHERE email == NULL
- WHERE email EQUALS NULL
? NULL means unknown, so comparisons with = never match. You must use IS NULL.

@ Queries and Filtering | I
Which LIKE pattern matches every name that starts with Ra?
= 'Ra%'
- '%Ra'
- 'Ra_'
- '*Ra*'
? % matches any number of characters. '%Ra' would match names that end with Ra, and _ matches exactly one character.

@ Queries and Filtering | I
What does SELECT DISTINCT city FROM customers return?
= Each different city value once
- Every city including duplicates
- Only the first row of the table
- The number of cities
? DISTINCT removes duplicate rows from the result.

@ DDL and DML | B
Which statement changes the values of existing rows?
= UPDATE
- INSERT
- ALTER
- MODIFY
? UPDATE ... SET changes data in existing rows. INSERT adds rows and ALTER changes table structure.

@ DDL and DML | I
What is the difference between DELETE and TRUNCATE?
= DELETE can remove selected rows with WHERE, while TRUNCATE removes all rows
- TRUNCATE can use a WHERE clause but DELETE cannot
- They are identical in every way
- DELETE drops the table structure
? DELETE supports a WHERE clause. TRUNCATE quickly empties the whole table and keeps its structure.

@ Aggregation | B
Which function returns the average of a numeric column?
= AVG
- MEAN
- AVERAGE
- MID
? AVG(column) calculates the arithmetic mean of the non-NULL values.

@ Aggregation | I
What does COUNT(column_name) count?
= The rows where that column is not NULL
- All rows including those with NULL
- Only the distinct values
- The number of columns in the table
? COUNT(*) counts rows, while COUNT(column) ignores NULLs in that column.

@ Aggregation | I
What does this query return?
  SELECT department, COUNT(*) FROM employees GROUP BY department HAVING COUNT(*) > 5;
= Departments that have more than 5 employees, with their counts
- Every department with its count
- Employees whose department number is above 5
- A syntax error
? GROUP BY forms one group per department and HAVING keeps only the groups with more than 5 rows.

@ Keys and Constraints | B
Which constraint guarantees that all values in a column are different?
= UNIQUE
- CHECK
- DEFAULT
- NOT NULL
? UNIQUE prevents duplicate values. NOT NULL only prevents missing values.

@ Keys and Constraints | I
What does a FOREIGN KEY enforce?
= A value must match an existing key in the referenced table
- Every value in the column must be unique
- The column can never be NULL
- The column value is generated automatically
? Foreign keys maintain referential integrity between related tables.

@ Subqueries | I
Which query returns employees whose salary is above the company average?
= SELECT * FROM employees WHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees);
- SELECT * FROM employees WHERE salary > AVG(salary);
- SELECT * FROM employees HAVING salary > AVG(salary);
- SELECT * FROM employees WHERE AVG(salary) < salary;
? Aggregate functions cannot be used directly in WHERE. A scalar subquery computes the average first.

@ Sorting and Limiting | A
Which query returns the second highest distinct salary?
= SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees);
- SELECT salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary LIMIT 2;
- SELECT SECOND(salary) FROM employees;
- SELECT MAX(salary) - 1 FROM employees;
? The inner query finds the highest salary and the outer query takes the maximum among the smaller values.

@ Query Execution | A
In which logical order does SQL evaluate the main clauses of a SELECT statement?
= FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY
- SELECT, FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, ORDER BY
- FROM, SELECT, WHERE, GROUP BY, ORDER BY, HAVING
- WHERE, FROM, GROUP BY, SELECT, HAVING, ORDER BY
? Rows are read (FROM), filtered (WHERE), grouped, filtered by group (HAVING), projected (SELECT) and finally sorted.
